Let's stream live sensor telemetry straight into your computer's screen using the Arduino IDE **Serial Monitor**!

### The Sensor Reader Sketch:
Upload the following sketch to read both the LDR on `A0` and the Ultrasonic distance sensor on pins `9` & `10`:

```cpp
const int trigPin = 9;   // HC-SR04 Trigger pin
const int echoPin = 10;  // HC-SR04 Echo pin
const int ldrPin = A0;   // Photoresistor analog pin

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);     // Open serial channel at 9600 baud
  pinMode(trigPin, OUTPUT); 
  pinMode(echoPin, INPUT);  
}

void loop() {
  // 1. Trigger ultrasonic acoustic chirp
  digitalWrite(trigPin, LOW);
  delayMicroseconds(2);
  digitalWrite(trigPin, HIGH);
  delayMicroseconds(10);
  digitalWrite(trigPin, LOW);

  // 2. Measure pulse duration in microseconds
  long duration = pulseIn(echoPin, HIGH);
  int distanceCm = duration * 0.034 / 2;

  // 3. Read analog light level
  int lightLevel = analogRead(ldrPin);

  // 4. Stream telemetry to Serial Monitor
  Serial.print("Light Level: ");
  Serial.print(lightLevel);
  Serial.print(" | Distance: ");
  Serial.print(distanceCm);
  Serial.println(" cm");

  delay(200); // 5 Hz telemetry cadence for easy reading
}
```

### Experiment With Telemetry:
Open the Serial Monitor (Ctrl+Shift+M / Cmd+Shift+M) and ensure the baud rate dropdown is set to **9600 baud**. Move your hand toward the ultrasonic sensor and watch the distance count drop smoothly. Cover the LDR with your thumb and watch the light level drop!

Now let's complete the loop by connecting a sensor input directly to a physical output actuator!

### Hardware Mission: Proximity Alarm LED
Keep your HC-SR04 ultrasonic sensor wired on pins `9` (TRIG) and `10` (ECHO). Add an indicator LED:
- Connect the **Anode** (longer leg) of a Red LED to Uno Digital Pin **13** (which has an onboard series resistor).
- Connect the **Cathode** (shorter flat leg) of the LED to the **GND** rail.

### Firmware Architecture:
Here is how sensor readings drive output decisions:

```cpp
const int trigPin = 9;
const int echoPin = 10;
const int alertLedPin = 13;
const int NEAR_THRESHOLD_CM = 15; // Trigger alarm if closer than 15 cm

void setup() {
  pinMode(trigPin, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(echoPin, INPUT);
  pinMode(alertLedPin, OUTPUT);
  Serial.begin(9600);
}

void loop() {
  // Ping ultrasonic sensor
  digitalWrite(trigPin, LOW);
  delayMicroseconds(2);
  digitalWrite(trigPin, HIGH);
  delayMicroseconds(10);
  digitalWrite(trigPin, LOW);

  long duration = pulseIn(echoPin, HIGH);
  int distanceCm = duration * 0.034 / 2;

  // Threshold decision logic
  if (distanceCm > 0 && distanceCm < NEAR_THRESHOLD_CM) {
    digitalWrite(alertLedPin, HIGH); // Alarm ON: Monster eye glows!
    Serial.println("WARNING: Intruder in perimeter!");
  } else {
    digitalWrite(alertLedPin, LOW);  // Alarm OFF: Area clear
    Serial.println("Status: Patrol normal.");
  }

  delay(100);
}
```

Upload the sketch, wave your hand in front of the sensor, and observe how your monster instantly reacts to proximity!

Living creatures do not rely on just one sense. A monster guards its territory using both sight and hearing simultaneously!

### Multi-Sensor Fusion:
When you combine multiple sensors, your robot can make sophisticated, multi-factor decisions using C++ boolean logic:

#### 1. Logical AND (`&&`) — Both Conditions Required:
Suppose we want a **Night-Watch Sentry**: the alarm should only wake up if it is pitch dark in the room **AND** someone sneaks within 20 cm of the guard post!

```cpp
if (lightLevel < 250 && distanceCm < 20) {
  digitalWrite(alarmLed, HIGH); // Intruder lurking in the shadows!
} else {
  digitalWrite(alarmLed, LOW);
}
```

#### 2. Logical OR (`||`) — Either Condition Triggers:
Suppose we want an **Emergency Security Alarm**: trigger if an object is dangerously close **OR** if an intruder turns on a flashlight:

```cpp
if (distanceCm < 10 || lightLevel > 800) {
  digitalWrite(alarmLed, HIGH); // Emergency breach!
}
```

### Prioritized Conditions:
What if sensor readings conflict? By ordering `if`, `else if`, and `else` blocks strategically, you establish clear priority hierarchy so safety rules always take precedence over everyday tasks!
